{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"1990s\" \"american music\" grunge \"hip hop\")"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1126222770#13_2300813379","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1918","rank":1,"snippet":"\" , \"end_char\": 7527.000000 , \"headings\": \"A Simple Guide to American Music History A Simple Guide to American Music History Roots of American music African American music Appalachian folk music Popular American music Pop origins Jazz and blues influence on pop Rock n roll Rap/hip hop Latin Rock in the 80s and beyond Folk/country American music Early country Gospel Doo wop Bakersfield sound Classical <cover>American music First New England school Second New England school 20 th century Conclusion \" , \"segment\": \"The 1990s saw rock music evolve into grunge, popularized by Nirvana, Soundgarden and others, which quickly gave way to mainstream alternative rock. Hip hop </cover>continued rising in popularity – in the mid-90s, sensations Tupac and Notorious B.I.G. were murdered. Like alternative rock, this music that was once on the fringe saturated the mainstream. Singer-songwriters became popular again (like in the early ‘70s) with artists like Alanis Morissette. Ska-influenced pop punk gave us Sublime and No Doubt. By the turn of the century, pop groups were very popular – Backstreet Boys, NSync, Spice Girls. Some of this music was Latin-"}}
