{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1918"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1126222770#12_2300811468","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1918","rank":2,"snippet":"in the 80s and beyond Then we hit the 1980s, with glam metal and punk rock continuing in popularity, further fracturing in alternative rock and hardcore styles. Hip hop became more mainstream toward the end of the 1980s with artists like LL Cool J. Defining albums were It Takes a Nation of Millions to Hold Us Back, and Straight Outta Compton. These albums were controversial because of their violent and vulgar lyrics. The <cover>1990s saw rock music evolve into grunge, popularized by Nirvana, Soundgarden and others, which quickly gave way to mainstream alternative rock. Hip hop continued rising in popularity – in the mid-90s, sensations Tupac and Notorious B.I.G. were murdered. Like alternative rock, this music that was once on the fringe saturated the mainstream. Singer-songwriters became popular again (like in the early ‘70s) with artists like Alanis Morissette. Ska-influenced pop punk gave us Sublime and No Doubt.\" , \"start_char\": 5875.000000 , \"title\": \"A Simple Guide to American Music </cover>History - PianoTV.net\" , \"url\": \"https://www.pianotv.net/2018/07/a-simple-guide-to-american-music-history/\" }"}}
